Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero x86/x64 optical boot image

iso.zero constructs ISO 9660 records and the El Torito BIOS/EFI catalog in
Zero itself. It is compiled by our --host target:
build/zero --host < iso.zero > build/iso
chmod +x build/iso

The same optical ISO offers
a BIOS dispatcher, AMD64 UEFI fallback loader, and IA32 UEFI fallback loader.
It is not a hybrid USB image or legacy MBR installer.
This is the only disk-image format distributed in the current release.
The FAT16 BOOT.IMG stored inside the ISO is its standard El Torito UEFI boot
payload. Separate .img staging files are internal build intermediates, not
downloads or renamed ISO files. Writable virtual hard disks are created and
managed separately by the user; the optical ISO cannot store saved files.

Input contract

The writer reads exactly five payloads concatenated in this order:

    16384 bytes     compiled BIOS stage, zero-padded to 16 KiB
    491520 bytes    x86-64 raw kernel, zero-padded to480KiB
    491520 bytes    x86 raw kernel, zero-padded to480KiB
    16777216 bytes  FAT16 system.img generated by image.zero
    1048576 bytes   TOOLS.BIN generated by bundle.zero

Total input length is 18825216 bytes. Reject actual binaries larger than their
slots before padding. The normal build.sh prepares inputs and runs the tool.
The FAT image contains both EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI and EFI/BOOT/BOOTIA32.EFI,
plus KERNEL.BIN, KERNEL32.BIN and TOOLS.BIN. Its UEFI loaders load their matching kernel
and compiler bundle from FAT. The BIOS dispatcher reads the separately stored matching kernel
from optical LBA40 or280; it does not need to parse FAT or ISO directories.

All payload bytes are copied unchanged. The FAT geometry and signature are
checked when that payload arrives. The separate bundle must start with
ZRBNDL01, version1, size1048576 and app count6. BIOS and kernel machine bytes are opaque
to the container writer; the compiler supplies the BIOS header/signature.
The tool reserves 4096 bytes, handles partial I/O and retries EINTR. Metadata
and earlier payloads may already have been written when an error occurs.
Only use output after exit status 0. Status 1 means input read error, 2 invalid
FAT/bundle header or short input, 3 excess input, 4 output write error. A closed output pipe
can still trigger the host's SIGPIPE behavior.

On-disc layout

All addresses here use2048-byte ISO logical sectors. Output is9224 sectors,
18890752 bytes. Unused sectors, padding and directory-record slack are zero.

    0..15      ISO system area
    16         Primary Volume Descriptor, volume identifier ZERO_SYSTEM
    17         El Torito Boot Record Volume Descriptor
    18         Volume Descriptor Set Terminator
    19         reserved
    20         root directory: dot, dot-dot and five sorted files
    21         mandatory little-endian root path table
    22         mandatory big-endian root path table
    23         El Torito boot catalog
    24..31     reserved
    32..39     BOOT.BIN;1, 16384-byte BIOS stage
    40..279    KERNEL.BIN;1, 491520-byte x86-64 raw kernel slot
    280..519   KERNEL32.BIN;1, 491520-byte x86 raw kernel slot
    520..8711  BOOT.IMG;1, 16777216-byte FAT16 image
    8712..9223 TOOLS.BIN;1, 1048576-byte native compiler bundle

Root file records are ordered BOOT.BIN, BOOT.IMG, KERNEL.BIN, KERNEL32.BIN,
TOOLS.BIN.
ISO quantities have the required little/big/paired encodings. Timestamps are
fixed at 1980-01-01 00:00 UTC, making equal inputs produce equal output.

El Torito catalog

The validation entry has platform 00 (x86 BIOS), a zero-sum 16-bit checksum,
and 55AA signature. Its default boot entry is bootable, uses no emulation,
and loads 32 sectors in 512-byte units from ISO LBA 32. Load segment 0 selects
0000:7c00. The BIOS program selects 32-bit protected mode or 64-bit long mode
according to the guest CPU and loads the appropriate480KiB kernel slot.

The final-section header is 91 with platform EF and one EFI boot entry.
That entry uses no emulation and32768 sectors in512-byte units at ISO LBA520.
Its FAT16 image contains both architecture-specific UEFI fallback paths;
firmware chooses the path corresponding to its own architecture.
El Torito counts use 512-byte units; start addresses use 2048-byte ISO LBAs.

The ISO is read-only. Persistent files need the separate writable data disk.

Historical container verification (before the384KiB slot growth)
xorriso independently recognized both catalog entries and reported all four
file sizes/LBAs exactly. Every extracted payload matched its input. Short,
excess and invalid-FAT input and /dev/full errors gave expected statuses.
Fragmented 97-byte input produced byte-identical output. Synthetic payloads
were used for these container tests; actual firmware/CPU boot checks belong
to the final integrated release report in verification.ztext.

Historical384KiB enlarged-layout verification is recorded in
verification/boot-growth-0.7.0/results.ztext, including all four firmware/CPU
paths, every payload roundtrip, catalog inspection and exact slot-tail probes.

The historical 448 KiB capacity-only probes remain in
verification/boot-capacity-448k/. Current 480 KiB slots and the native compiler
bundle are checked in verification/guest-bundle-480k/; the probe verifies the
whole 1 MiB loaded bundle, every 8 MiB workspace page and the final kernel
byte on BIOS/UEFI, both 32- and 64-bit. Native compiler execution evidence is
separate in verification/guest-app/bundle/. Final OS integration results
remain separate in verification.ztext.
